{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "deborah-palm",
  "text": "A woman is running an entire nation from under a tree and the general will not go to war without her.\n\nJudges chapter four. Deborah. Prophetess. And the text says plainly, she judged Israel at that time. Not helped. Judged.\n\nShe holds court under a palm tree between Ramah and Bethel, and the children of Israel come up to her for judgment. That is the office. Written down. No hedging.\n\nSo she summons Barak, the military commander, and gives him God's orders. Take ten thousand men to mount Tabor.\n\nAnd Barak, a grown general, says, \"If thou wilt go with me, then I will go. But if thou wilt not go with me, then I will not go.\"\n\nSir.\n\nShe goes. Of course she goes. But she calls it first: \"The journey that thou takest shall not be for thine honour, for the Lord shall sell Sisera into the hand of a woman.\"\n\nShe told him exactly how it would end. And that is exactly how it ended, in a tent, with another woman named Jael.\n\nDeborah didn't just judge. She called the shot, went anyway, and let the record show who did the work.\n\nPalm tree. Whole nation. No notes.",
